From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Richard Spindler <richard@lateralblast.com.au>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 55/55] scp: answer with a match status when lseek fails in the
 -Z source

After a hash match the source seeks the open file to the end of the part the
sink already has. If that lseek failed it jumped to the next file without
sending the match status the sink was waiting for, so the first byte of the
next file's record was read as the status and the sink lost its place.

Treat a failed seek as a mismatch: reset the position, say "F" and send the
whole file, which the sink handles like any other mismatch.
---
 scp.c | 15 ++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/scp.c b/scp.c
index 62ea45808..bdaea6a6f 100644
--- a/scp.c
+++ b/scp.c
@@ -1717,9 +1717,18 @@ syserr:			run_err("%s: %s", name, strerror(errno));
 #ifdef DEBUG
 							fprintf(stderr, "%s: lseek did not return %ld\n", hostname, insize) ;
 #endif
-							goto next;
-						}
-						match = "M";
+							/*
+							 * The sink is waiting for the match status, so
+							 * moving on to the next file here would make
+							 * the next record's first byte the status.
+							 * Say the fragments differ, and send the whole
+							 * file from the start.
+							 */
+							(void) lseek(fd, 0, SEEK_SET);
+							match = "F";
+							xfer_size = stb.st_size;
+						} else
+							match = "M";
 					} else {
 						/* the fragments don't match so we should start over from the begining */
 #ifdef DEBUG
-- 
